The test case `tests/lib/devicetree/devices` is failing due to an incorrect expected device initialization order in the `test_init_order` function. The Zephyr kernel initializes devices based on their initialization level, then their priority, while respecting dependencies. If a device `A` depends on device `B`, `B` will always be initialized before `A` within the same initialization level.

In this test case, all devices in the `POST_KERNEL` level have their dependencies on devices with lower or equal initialization priorities. For example, `TEST_DEVA` (priority 21) depends on `TEST_I2C` (priority 20). This means that the initialization scheduler can initialize all devices in a single pass, strictly following the priority order.

The existing test code expects a different, non-priority-based order, which is inconsistent with the current device initialization logic. This causes an assertion failure and a test crash.

The fix is to update the assertions in `test_init_order` to reflect the correct, priority-based initialization sequence of the test devices.

